  "strategy": "Application and Rationale:\nOur approach leverages discrete cosine transform (DCT) coefficients for balanced representation, which maintains smoothness constraints without direct discretization. The objective function is optimized through coordinate-wise updates, which systematically explores the solution space across multiple time scales. This method aims to surpass the parent solution's C value by ensuring global exploration while satisfying integral and bound constraints. The core breakthrough is the use of DCT basis functions to model h(x) implicitly, avoiding premature discretization that trapped gradient-based methods in local optima.\n\nMathematical Specification:\n- Representation: h(x) = a_0/2 + sum_{k=1}^{N/2} a_k * cos(k\u03c0x/2) * \u03b1^k\n- Parameters: Nonnegative coefficients a_k (k=0,...,N_dct)\n- Constraint: \u222bh dx = 1 exactly \u2192 linear constraint in coefficients\n- Objective: C_5 = (1/2) * max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx (discrete via DCT)\n- Variables: DCT coefficients with bounds: 0 \u2264 a_0; others unrestricted\n- Critical feature: Smoothness enforced through exponential decay factor \u03b1 (target ~0.8)\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n1. Validate n_points (initial 190 samples)\n2. Initialize DCT basis functions up to max_freq (N_max = 15)\n3. Define parent solution: Repair initial guess to satisfy \u222bh=1 using coefficient adjustment\n4. Optimization loop:\n   a. Transform coefficient vector (a) to time domain h via DCT\n   b. Score using: C = max_k [\u222bh(1-h(-k))dx] (utilizing orthogonality)\n   c. Coordinate-wise update with Armijo backtracking\n5. Repair: Adjust integral while preserving DCT structure if needed\n6. Upsampling: Linear interpolation then integral repair\n\nSearch Choices and variations:\n- N_max: Starts at 15, if plateau (\u0394C < 0.0001) \u2192 increase to 30\n- \u03b1 (exponential envelope): Starts at 0.8, adapt between 0.65-0.95\n- Learning-rate scheduling: Initial decay 0.95 per epoch until stall\n- Budget splits: 50% exploration, 20% validation, 30% local repair\n- Fallbacks: If score stagnates, revert to parent solution (not unilaterally)\n\nNumerical Robustness and Memory Use:\n- Special repair: Integral is maintained exactly by linear adjustment to lowest mode\n- DCT precision: Use double floating point (15\u219230 digits)\n- Memory: Store only current solution (O(N_max))\n- Convergence: 200\u00d7 basis function updates unless time expires\n- Fallback: Return parent if any DCT basis yields invalid domain\n\nRuntime Safeguards:\n- Wall-clock timer: Budget halved in power-law decay per DCT mode\n- Termination triggers: Budget<30s OR C_5 improvement<10^-6\n- Time recovery: Restart with reduced N_max if any progress stalls\n- Fallback: Keep parent solution unless improved by <0.1%\n- Score calculation: Explicit use of DCT orthogonality for faster correlation\n\nReturn Contract:\n- Format: (h_values (as interpolated), float(C_5), int(n_points))\n- Validation: Force h(x) to [0,1] through DCT coefficient scaling\n- Repair: Integral maintenance via highest mode clamping (when needed)\n- All variables: N_max \u2264 30 (hence integral/floating point manageable)\n\nBudget Split: First 40% full-scale optimization (N_max=15), next 30% evaluation at boundaries (explicit min/max forcing), last 30% local search using gradient projection.\n\nFulfill the function call and timing constraints by packaging the DCT formulation as separate computational steps with explicit time allocations. The coder must ensure smoothness enforcement is mathematically exact (not via chance-integral adjustment), and that optimizations converge to constrained minima. Use of DCT inverses and orthogonality in objective calculation is mandatory.",
